      <p>Most accounts I open have the same problem: somebody is spending real money against numbers that were never accurate. Conversion tracking double counts, brand searches are reported as new demand, and Performance Max quietly takes credit for sales that would have happened anyway. The reports look fine. The bank balance does not.</p>
      <p>An audit is how you find out which of those is happening in your account, before changing anything.</p>

      <h2>What the $100 analysis checks</h2>
      <p>The free audit tells you which of these areas have issues. The paid analysis goes through every one in depth and gives you the solution for each problem it finds. The order matters. Tracking first, because every other finding depends on whether the conversion data is real. The reasoning behind each check is set out in <a href="/learn/what-a-google-ads-audit-should-check/">what a Google Ads audit should actually check</a>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Conversion tracking, end to end.</strong> Every conversion action, how it fires, whether it double counts, whether values are passed, and whether GA4 and Google Ads agree with each other.</li>
        <li><strong>Brand versus non-brand.</strong> Separated and reported apart, so you can see what the account genuinely added rather than what it collected.</li>
        <li><strong>Search terms, as far back as the data goes.</strong> The exact queries you paid for, and the categories nobody chose to buy.</li>
        <li><strong>Account structure.</strong> Campaign and ad group architecture, match types, negative lists, and whether budgets are pacing against the right things.</li>
        <li><strong>Performance Max.</strong> Asset group structure, brand exclusions, audience signals, and the channel breakdown Google does not show by default.</li>
        <li><strong>Shopping and the product feed.</strong> Titles, attributes, disapprovals and availability, because feed problems look like bidding problems.</li>
        <li><strong>Bidding.</strong> Strategy choice, target settings, and whether the account has enough conversion volume for the strategy it is using.</li>
        <li><strong>Landing pages.</strong> Not a redesign, a check on whether the page answers the search that paid to get there.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <p>A written document, in plain language, with the findings ordered by how much money each one is costing. Not a sixty page PDF of screenshots.</p>
      <p>Each finding says what the problem is, what it is costing, and exactly how to fix it, in priority order. Then a walkthrough call to go through it and answer questions.</p>
      <p>The document is yours. If you want to hand it to your current agency or fix things in house, that is a perfectly good outcome.</p>

      <h2>What it costs and how long it takes</h2>
      <p>The free audit costs nothing, carries no obligation and lists the issues in your account. The full account analysis is $100, one time, adds the solution for each issue, and is delivered within 3 days of getting access.</p>
      <p>Read-only access is enough to start. I do not need to make changes to review the account, and I will not make any without asking.</p>

      <h2>Why the full analysis is paid</h2>
      <p>A thorough analysis takes days of work, and charging for it keeps the incentive clean. I am being paid for the analysis, not for the retainer that might follow, so the honest answer is available. Sometimes that answer is that the account is fine and the problem is the offer, or the landing page, or that Google Ads is not the right channel at all.</p>
      <p>Not sure it is worth $100 yet? Start with the <a href="#enquiry">free audit</a>. It lists the issues in your account, and the paid analysis is there if you want the solutions.</p>

      <h2>Common findings</h2>
      <p>These turn up often enough that I check for them before anything else. There is more detail on <a href="/#findings">the things I look for first</a>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The same purchase counted twice, from a hard-coded tag and a GA4 import</li>
        <li>A thank-you page conversion that fires again on every refresh</li>
        <li>Broad match with a negative list that was built once and never revisited</li>
        <li>One conversion action counting newsletter signups and signed contracts equally</li>
        <li>Performance Max with no brand exclusions, absorbing demand you already had</li>
        <li>A product feed using manufacturer titles nobody searches for</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who it suits</h2>
      <p>Accounts already spending, where somebody suspects the numbers but cannot prove it. If you are not running Google Ads yet there is nothing to audit, and a <a href="/#contact">conversation about whether to start</a> is more useful.</p>
      <p>It is also the right first step before any management engagement. I do not quote on an account I have not seen, and I do not move live budget in week one.</p>

      <h2>What happens next</h2>
      <p>Send read-only access and one line about what you want the account to do. I will confirm what I can see, agree the scope, and come back with the document.</p>
      <p>If the account turns out to need management rather than a one-off fix, <a href="/#pricing">pricing is published</a> and the analysis fee is not a deposit on anything.</p>
